<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Agrippa II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 27–c. 93</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Herod Agrippa II, Marcus Julius Agrippa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Agrippa II was the last ruler of the Herodian dynasty, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-agrippa-i">Herod Agrippa I</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> gave him the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> in about 50 and later larger territories in the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>. He sided with Rome in the Jewish revolt of 66 to 70, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles">Acts of the Apostles</a> records him hearing the apostle Paul at Caesarea.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agrippa-ii.jpg" alt="Agrippa II" width="320" height="154" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agrippa_II_Judaea_Capta_91000679.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Herod Agrippa II, whose Roman name was Marcus Julius Agrippa, was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-agrippa-i">Herod Agrippa I</a> and the brother of Berenice, Mariamne and Drusilla. He was raised and educated at the court of the Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>. He was only 17 when his father died, so Claudius kept him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and sent Cuspius Fadus to govern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a> as procurator. In Rome Agrippa spoke up for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to Claudius, against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaritans">Samaritans</a> and the procurator Ventidius Cumanus, who was thought to have caused disturbances.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-of-chalcis">Herod of Chalcis</a> died his small Syrian realm of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> was given to Agrippa, with the right to superintend the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a> and appoint its high priest, but only as tetrarch. In 53 he gave up Chalcis and received the title of king over the territories previously governed by Philip, including Iturea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trachonitis">Trachonitis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banias">Paneas</a>, and over the kingdom of Lysanias in Abila. In 55 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> and Taricheae in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>, and Livias in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peraea">Peraea</a>. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-the-apostle">Paul the Apostle</a> pleaded his case before Agrippa and Berenice at Caesarea Maritima, probably in 59 or 60.</p><p>Agrippa spent large sums beautifying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and other cities, especially Berytus, and his partiality for Berytus made him unpopular with his subjects. In 65 or 66 he tried to prevent war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and urged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to endure the abuses of the procurator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gessius-florus">Gessius Florus</a>, but he failed, and the citizens of Jerusalem expelled him and Berenice in 66. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-jewish-roman-war">First Jewish–Roman War</a> he sent 2,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and cavalry to Vespasian, accompanied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-saint">Titus</a> and was wounded at the siege of Gamla. After the capture of Jerusalem he went to Rome, where he was made a praetor and given more territory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> was on intimate terms with Agrippa, who supplied information for his Antiquities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, and Josephus preserved two of Agrippa's letters. According to the patriarch Photius, Agrippa died childless at 70, in the third year of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>, which is 100, but Josephus's statements and contemporary inscriptions from his kingdom cast serious doubt on this, and the modern scholarly consensus holds that he died before 93 or 94. He was the last ruler of the House of Herod.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Herod Agrippa II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After his death in 48, Chalcis went to his nephew <b>Agrippa II</b>, who exchanged it in 53 for larger lands further south.</blockquote><a href="/state/herodian-kingdom-of-chalcis">Herodian Kingdom of Chalcis</a></li><li><blockquote>His son <b>Agrippa II</b> received Chalcis and from 53 the northern lands, renaming Caesarea Philippi as Neronias.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-herod-agrippa">Kingdom of Herod Agrippa</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=69" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 672 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Agrippa II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 942h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=69">What was here in 69 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
